== The Dalmar Dynasty ==

=== Rise ===
The Dalmar Dynasty emerged from the military aristocracy around [[Lake Haro]] approximately a century after the fall of the [[Geedi Period|Geedi]]. '''[[Garaad]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "wisdom"), the founding king, reconsolidated the Ufyen state but adopted a different approach from his predecessors. Rather than governing the entire coast directly, the Dalmar kings focused on controlling the mountain passes, the Upper [[Buran River]] valley, and the [[Kushish]] iron mines, extracting revenue through tariffs.

=== The tariff system ===
The Dalmar kings maintained their capital at [[Badalga-Jir]] and fortified [[Jowhar]] as the kingdom's principal customs post, establishing a permanent garrison to control river traffic on the Upper Buran. This system produced a period of tense equilibrium: the coastal cities were nominally independent but economically dependent on highland iron and timber, while Ufyen depended on the coast for Mesogean gold, silver, and luxury goods.

The '''[[Xarigii Cashuurta]]''' ("Tariff Conflict", ~300–280 BCE), in which Ufyen imposed a complete embargo on river traffic for approximately two decades, was the most severe of these crises.

=== Administration ===
The Dalmar period produced the earliest surviving [[Mesogean script|Echian script]] inscriptions from the Ufyen highlands. Royal decrees, trade agreements, and legal codes were inscribed in stone at [[Badalga-Jir]]. These inscriptions provide the first written evidence for the internal politics of the Ufyen state, including references to the [[Geedi Period|Geedi]], the [[Hiddid]], and the [[Bariites]].

=== End of the Dalmar Dynasty ===
'''[[Timacade]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "white-haired") was the last king of the Dalmar Dynasty. By the late 3rd century BCE, the dynasty faced renewed [[Bariites|Bariite]] pressure from the east and [[Dakari people|Dakari]] raids from the north. The Dalmar were succeeded by the Barre Dynasty around 270 BCE.

== The Barre Dynasty ==

The '''Barre Dynasty''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Barre'', "teaching/instruction") succeeded the Dalmar around 270 BCE and continued the chokepoint strategy while expanding Echian literacy within the highland administration. Under the Barre, the use of written records extended beyond the royal court to the ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' garrison commanders and the Jowhar customs post.

The Barre period ended around 150 BCE under the same pattern of eastern and northern military pressure that had ended earlier dynasties, leading to the rise of the [[Warfaa Period]].

